      perfectionism: [66, 0.58, "d", "D"],
      // [src_renoir_duret1906, src_renoir_riviere1921, src_renoir_vollard1925 | 1850s and 1875-1883 | critic-collector + friend + self-testimony] When machine printing ended hand porcelain painting, Renoir turned to decorating fans, café walls and painted window blinds; Duret says the blind work paid well enough to fund study at Gleyre's studio, and Riviere adds that he never asked his parents for help. In the 1870s, after the Drouot sales of 1875 and 1877 raised only 2,150 and 2,005 francs, Duret says he stopped holding such sales, turned to portrait commissions from Choquet, Charpentier and the Berard family, and re-entered the Salon in 1879; Riviere confirms he sent works to the Salon and stayed out of the group shows of 1879-81, and Duret that he rejoined them in 1882. Two distinct changes of course documented by three perspectives, each told largely from Renoir's own report of his motives.
